Sure? I've got one of these dongles that a friend in the US sent to me,
although I've never got around to doing anything useful with it. Anyway,
as I recall the legal stuff said that you were bound by the terms on
opening the software - so people threw the software away unopened and kept
their new shiny toy. 
My plan was to make it automatically compile shopping lists - living in
the kind of house that has PCs in the kitchen, it wouldn't be too tricky
to tuck the PC away somewhere, dangle the barcode scanner somewhere
useful, and just scan stuff before binning it. You could then take it
further, and scan stuff in when you buy it, so it knows what you've got in
your cupboards. If you know what you've got in your cupboards, it can
probably tell you what things you can cook, and it's suddenly immensely
useful. 
The main thing that stopped me coding it up was the problem with
representing quantities in recipies and on food. It would have to store a
number and a unit, and know how to convert between units - so it would
know, say, that if I told it I've got 3 tomatoes I've really got 100g of
tomatoes, and so on. Oh well.

